Title: a LAMBDA evaluated in a loop is compiled once PER ITERATION at a low threshold
Status: open
Found: 2.0.23, while open-coding LAMBDA and FUNCTION (compiler.issue slice 21)

  Renamed from `...never compiles': measuring it showed the opposite
  failure, and a worse one. Both regimes are below.

SYMPTOM

  (repeat 60000 (setq acc (+ acc (apply (lambda (x) (car x)) (list p)))))

measured against the same loop calling CAR directly (the control):

  workload                 interpreted   compiled
  control (car directly)         20 ms       4 ms
  hof     ((function car))       32 ms      12 ms
  lam     (a lambda per pass)    60 ms   26416 ms      <-- 440x SLOWER

Compiling this loop makes it four hundred times slower than
interpreting it. Nothing warns; it just stops.

CAUSE

Compilation is decided per FUNCTION OBJECT, by AUTOLISP-USUBR's
CALL-COUNT: MAYBE-COMPILE-USUBR weaves the fork on the call that
crosses *AUTOLISP-COMPILATION-THRESHOLD*. Sound for a DEFUN, whose
object outlives the decision.

EVAL-LAMBDA-FORM builds a NEW usubr every time the LAMBDA form is
EVALUATED, and a LAMBDA inside a loop body is evaluated once per
iteration. So the decision is retaken from scratch 60000 times, on
60000 objects that are all the same code:

  threshold 1   -- each fresh closure crosses on its first call, so the
                   HOST COMPILER RUNS ONCE PER ITERATION. This is the
                   440x row above.
  threshold 16  -- the shipped default. Each closure is called once,
                   reaches 1, and is discarded, so the body is NEVER
                   compiled however hot the loop is. No cliff, but no
                   compilation either.

Neither is a wrong answer to the question CALL-COUNT asks. The question
is wrong: it measures the life of an OBJECT where what matters is the
heat of a SITE.

WHY THIS IS NOT THEORETICAL

Threshold 1 is what the TEST SUITE sets -- deliberately, so every
function a test exercises runs compiled (see the docstring on
*AUTOLISP-COMPILATION-THRESHOLD*). Any test that puts a lambda in a
loop therefore takes the 440x path. That is a plausible reading of a
future "the compiler tests got slow" report.

At SPEED 3 the eager path is NOT involved: COMPILE-USUBR-IF-EAGER is
called only from DEFUN, so a lambda never compiles at definition. This
is entirely the threshold path.

WHY SLICE 21 DID NOT FIX IT

Slice 21 open-codes the LAMBDA FORM -- the closure is built by a direct
call to EVAL-LAMBDA-FORM instead of through an interpreter dispatch --
and that is all. The A/B settles that this is OLDER than the slice: with
the slice's branches disabled the same row measures 24448 ms, against
26416 ms with them on -- the same pathology either way, the difference
being run-to-run noise on a 25-second measurement. Slice 21 did not
cause it and does not cure it; it is the first thing to have MEASURED
it. Fixing it is a lifetime question, not a translation one, and did
not belong in a slice about translation.

POSSIBLE FIXES, none chosen

  1. Key the count on the lambda's SOURCE FORM rather than on the
     usubr, so every closure from one site shares one identity and one
     decision. The count then means "how often this site ran", which is
     the question the threshold wants answered. Cheapest; fixes both
     regimes at once.

  2. Compile the body once at transpile time and attach the ready-made
     COMPILED-BODY to each new usubr. No threshold involved, but it
     pays compilation for a lambda that may run once -- exactly what
     the threshold exists to prevent.

  3. Cache the closure on the enclosing usubr and reuse it. WRONG:
     AutoLISP closures capture the context, so two iterations can
     legitimately differ. That is a semantic change wearing an
     optimization's clothes.

(1) looks right and is small. Its one risk is identity -- two textually
identical lambdas at different sites must not share a count in a way
that makes one site's heat compile the other's. Harmless for
correctness, since the bodies are equal, but it would make the
measurement confusing, so key on the form OBJECT rather than on its
text.

GUARD WORTH HAVING EITHER WAY

MAYBE-COMPILE-USUBR has no protection against compiling equivalent code
repeatedly. Whatever fix is chosen, a compiler that can be invited to
run 60000 times by an ordinary loop should say so -- a counter, or a
warning past some absurd number of compilations in one run -- rather
than absorbing it silently as 26 seconds.

MEASUREMENT

`lam' in the slice-21 benchmark (.bench in the slice's commit message)
is this shape. Any fix should move that row and leave `control' alone.
